[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-46", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 46 — the driver runs LDBC's official schedule; IC05 admitted; first official row 1360.9 ops/s at TCR 0.00015", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-46.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 46 - the driver runs LDBC's own schedule; IC05 admitted; the first official-schedule row\n\n> Seed: iteration 45's S36. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn v2 (amended today:\n> `:schedule :official`); rung :sf1-full; SUT levi (M4 4P+6E, 16\n> threads, 25.4M quads), driver judah, LAN.\n\n## Generate / Rank\n\n  The official frequencies were fetched from the LDBC driver's own\n  sf1.properties (update_interleave 4477 sim-ms; IC02 every 37 updates,\n  IC05 every 57, IC07 87, IC08 45, IC09 157 ...). The driver gained\n  `--official`: each complex read type on its own clock at\n  interleave x freq x TCR; the update stream at the same TCR under the\n  dependency rule; short reads following a complex read onto the first\n  row's person and message ids with probability 1.0 then x 0.2 each (an\n  approximation of LdbcSnbShortReadGenerator; the official generator\n  walks more of the result -- stated). The sweep is over TCR; the metric\n  is the throughput at the lowest passing TCR, as the audited table\n  defines it. A first run showed 0 updates -- the stream was built only\n  when `--tcr` was given; fixed before any row.\n\n    schedule official, 64 slots     tcr      on-time%  thru/s   upd/s  complex  short\n                                    0.0004     99.03    625.5    536     943     905\n                                    0.0002    100.00   1252.3   1092    1888    1808\n                                    0.00015    96.18   1360.9   1475    2518    2402   <- the row\n                                    0.0001     30.84   1073.1   2407    3779    3608   FAILS\n\n  IC05 is in the mix (5 of 14 complex reads, 7/7 short, 8/8 inserts).\n  At the passing TCR every wire latency is inflated by queueing behind\n  IC05: 160 ms server p50 at 26/s is 4.2 thread-seconds per second on\n  16 threads, with 64 in flight. Read-after-write 462/0/0.\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - This row and the uniform 64-slot row (4631.8) measure different\n    things and are kept as different columns. The official mix is\n    mostly updates and short reads: at the passing point 1,475 upd/s +\n    126 complex/s + 120 short/s. The number is smaller and the shape is\n    the benchmark's.\n  - IC05 is now the schedule's wall in plain terms (S37): its 160 ms is\n    paid 26 times a second. The next lever is either IC05's plan again\n    or the host.\n  - The short-read generator is the weakest approximation in the row and\n    the one that moves the number most (S38): every short read is\n    ~0.2 ms and the official generator produces more of them.\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: a new column (`:schedule\n:official`) with its first row, 1360.9 ops/s at TCR 0.00015; IC05\nadmitted. Ratio to the audited No.1: 102 on throughput, at a 21x slower\nTCR (0.00315 at SF300).\n\nSeeds for iteration 47, in order:\n\n  - S37: IC05 under the schedule (160 ms x 26/s).\n  - S38: the official short-read generator, exactly.\n  - S34: bindings as arrays. Owner: a SUT host.\n"}]
